window.LB = window.LB || {};
LB["p6-habitats"] = {
  notes: [
    { h: "Organism, population and community", t: "An organism is a single living thing, such as one mudskipper. A population is a group of organisms of the same kind living together in the same place at the same time. A community is made up of different populations of organisms living together and interacting with one another in the same place.", kw: ["organism", "population", "same kind", "same place at the same time", "community", "different populations"] },
    { h: "Habitat and environment", t: "A habitat is the place where an organism lives, such as a pond, a mangrove swamp, a forest floor or a field. A habitat provides the organisms with food, water, shelter and a place to reproduce. The environment is the surroundings of an organism: its physical factors together with the other living things around it.", kw: ["habitat", "place where an organism lives", "food, water, shelter", "environment", "surroundings"] },
    { h: "Physical factors of the environment", t: "The physical (non-living) factors of an environment are light, temperature, water, air and mineral salts. Different habitats have different amounts of each factor: a forest floor is dim and damp, an open field is bright and hot, while a pond has water all the time. Changes in these factors can change which organisms can live there and how many of them there are.", kw: ["physical factors", "light", "temperature", "water", "air", "mineral salts"] },
    { h: "How physical factors affect where organisms live", t: "Organisms are found where the physical factors suit them. Green plants need light to make food, so most grow in bright places, while mosses and ferns grow well in shady, damp places. Woodlice and earthworms are found in dark, damp places such as under rotting logs, where they lose less water. In a fair test on light, only the amount of light is changed and all other factors are kept the same.", kw: ["light", "make food", "shady, damp places", "dark, damp places", "lose less water", "fair test"] },
    { h: "Interdependence", t: "Organisms in a community depend on one another. Plants provide food, oxygen and shelter for animals, while animals help plants by pollinating their flowers and dispersing their seeds, and give off carbon dioxide which plants use. A change in one population, such as the removal of a predator, affects other populations in the community.", kw: ["depend on one another", "food", "oxygen", "shelter", "pollinating", "dispersing seeds", "carbon dioxide"] },
    { h: "Competition and population size", t: "Organisms compete for resources that are limited: plants compete for light, water, mineral salts and space, while animals compete for food, water, space and mates. A population increases when there are more births than deaths, for example when there is more food or fewer predators. It decreases when there is less food, more predators, disease or stronger competition.", kw: ["compete", "light, water, mineral salts and space", "food, water, space and mates", "more births than deaths", "less food", "more predators"] },
    { h: "Adaptations: structural and behavioural", t: "An adaptation is a feature or behaviour that helps an organism to survive and reproduce in its habitat. A structural adaptation is a body part or body feature, such as the thick fur of a polar bear or the spines of a cactus. A behavioural adaptation is the way an organism acts, such as birds migrating or an opossum playing dead.", kw: ["adaptation", "survive", "structural adaptation", "body part", "behavioural adaptation", "the way an organism acts"] },
    { h: "Adaptations for obtaining food", t: "Birds have beaks suited to their food: an eagle has a hooked beak to tear flesh and a kingfisher has a long, sharp beak to catch fish. A butterfly has a long, coiled tube-like mouthpart to suck nectar, and a frog has a long, sticky tongue to catch insects. Plants also have adaptations: a water lily has large, flat, floating leaves to trap more light, and a pitcher plant traps insects to obtain mineral salts it cannot get enough of from the poor soil.", kw: ["hooked beak", "long sticky tongue", "large, flat leaves", "trap more light", "pitcher plant", "mineral salts"] },
    { h: "Escaping predators: camouflage and mimicry", t: "Camouflage lets an organism blend in with its surroundings so that predators cannot easily see it, for example a stick insect that looks like a twig. Mimicry is when a harmless organism looks like a harmful or poisonous one, such as a hoverfly that looks like a wasp, so predators avoid it. Other adaptations include hard shells (tortoise), sharp quills (porcupine), ink (squid), and behaviours such as playing dead or rolling into a ball.", kw: ["camouflage", "blend in with its surroundings", "mimicry", "harmless", "looks like a harmful", "predators avoid it"] },
    { h: "Surviving extreme conditions", t: "Desert: a cactus has spines instead of broad leaves to reduce water loss, a thick fleshy stem to store water and widespread roots to absorb water quickly when it rains; a camel has long eyelashes to keep sand out of its eyes, wide flat feet for walking on soft sand, and a hump that stores fat which can be used for energy. Many desert animals are nocturnal: they stay in burrows in the hot day and come out in the cool night. Polar: a polar bear has thick fur and a thick layer of fat to keep warm, penguins huddle together, and some animals hibernate or migrate to warmer places during winter.", kw: ["reduce water loss", "store water", "stores fat", "nocturnal", "thick layer of fat", "hibernate", "migrate"] },
    { h: "Breathing in water and mangrove adaptations", t: "Fish and tadpoles have gills to take in oxygen dissolved in water. Mosquito larvae and pupae have breathing tubes that reach the water surface to take in air, while whales and dolphins have lungs and must swim to the surface to breathe through their blowholes. Mangrove mud is waterlogged and has very little air: the Api-api tree has breathing roots that grow upwards out of the mud to take in oxygen from the air, and the Bakau tree has prop roots that support it in the soft mud.", kw: ["gills", "oxygen dissolved in water", "breathing tubes", "blowhole", "breathing roots", "prop roots", "waterlogged"] },
    { h: "Adaptations for reproduction and seed dispersal", t: "Flowers with bright petals, scent and nectar attract insects and birds for pollination. Fruits and seeds are adapted for dispersal: winged fruits like the Angsana are dispersed by wind, fibrous fruits like the coconut float in water, and juicy fruits are eaten by animals. Dispersal carries seeds away from the parent plant so that seedlings do not have to compete with it for light, water, mineral salts and space. The Bakau seed germinates while still on the parent tree, so the seedling can quickly take root in the soft mud when it drops.", kw: ["attract", "pollination", "dispersed by wind", "float", "away from the parent plant", "compete", "germinates while still on the parent tree"] }
  ],
  traps: [
    "‘A population is all the animals in a pond’ -> a population is organisms of the SAME kind; all the different populations together form a community.",
    "Plants are part of the community too -> a community includes every population in the habitat: plants, animals, fungi and bacteria.",
    "‘A camel’s hump stores water’ -> the hump stores FAT, which can be used for energy when food is scarce.",
    "Mixing up structural and behavioural -> a body part or feature is structural (a turtle’s shell); what the organism DOES is behavioural (the turtle pulling its head into the shell).",
    "‘Breathing roots take in water’ -> breathing roots grow out of the mud to take in OXYGEN from the air, because waterlogged mud has very little air; prop roots give SUPPORT.",
    "‘Whales and dolphins breathe with gills’ -> they have lungs and must come to the surface to take in air through their blowholes.",
    "Camouflage vs mimicry -> camouflage = blending in with the surroundings; mimicry = a harmless organism looking like a harmful one.",
    "‘Seeds are dispersed so the plant has more seeds’ -> dispersal carries seeds AWAY from the parent plant so the seedlings compete less for light, water, mineral salts and space."
  ],
  lessons: [
    {
      h: "Organism, population, community or habitat?",
      concept: "Count populations by counting KINDS of organisms, not individual animals: 30 guppies are ONE population. Everything living in the place together is ONE community, and the place itself is the habitat. Plants count as populations too, and each kind of plant is a separate population.",
      example: { q: "At a pond in Sungei Buloh, Mei saw 12 mudskippers, 8 fiddler crabs, 2 kingfishers, many Api-api trees and many Bakau trees. (a) How many populations did she see? (b) What makes up the community? (c) What is the habitat of the mudskippers?", marks: 3, think: ["List the KINDS: mudskipper, fiddler crab, kingfisher, Api-api, Bakau.", "Each kind is one population, however many individuals there are: 5 populations.", "All five populations living together and interacting form one community.", "The habitat is the place where they live: the pond / mangrove swamp."], answer: "(a) 5 populations. (b) All the mudskippers, fiddler crabs, kingfishers, Api-api trees and Bakau trees living together and interacting in the pond. (c) The pond in the mangrove swamp." },
      tip: "Watch for ‘all the fish’ when there are several kinds of fish: that is more than one population.",
      try: { q: "A tank contains 10 guppies, 5 goldfish, 3 snails and some Hydrilla plants. Which statement is correct?", o: ["There are 18 populations in the tank.", "All the fish in the tank form one population.", "There are 4 populations and 1 community in the tank.", "The guppies alone form a community."], a: 2, why: "Guppy, goldfish, snail and Hydrilla are 4 kinds, so 4 populations; together they form 1 community. The guppies and goldfish are different kinds, so ‘all the fish’ is 2 populations." }
    },
    {
      h: "Explaining a structural adaptation: feature → function → survival",
      concept: "Full marks need three links: name the FEATURE, say what it DOES, and say how that helps the organism SURVIVE in its habitat. ‘The cactus has spines so it can survive’ skips the middle link and loses marks.",
      example: { q: "A cactus has spines instead of broad leaves and a thick, fleshy stem. Explain how each feature helps it survive in the desert.", marks: 2, think: ["Feature 1: spines have a much smaller surface area than broad leaves.", "Function: less water is lost to the hot, dry air.", "Feature 2: a thick, fleshy stem stores water.", "Survival: the cactus has water to use during the long dry periods."], answer: "The spines reduce water loss from the cactus. The thick, fleshy stem stores water so the cactus can survive the long dry periods in the desert." },
      tip: "Always use the exact verbs markers look for: ‘reduce water loss’, ‘store water’, ‘take in oxygen’, ‘trap more light’, ‘keep warm’.",
      try: { q: "A polar bear has a thick layer of fat under its skin. How does this help it survive?", o: ["It stores water for the bear to drink.", "It reduces heat loss, keeping the bear warm in the cold.", "It helps the bear blend in with the snow.", "It helps the bear float so it does not need to swim."], a: 1, why: "The fat is a layer that reduces heat loss from the body. Blending in with snow is done by the white fur, not the fat." }
    },
    {
      h: "Breathing in water and in mangrove mud",
      concept: "Always name the structure AND where the oxygen comes from. Gills take in oxygen dissolved in the water. Breathing tubes, blowholes and mangrove breathing roots take in oxygen from the AIR above the water or mud. For mangroves, add WHY: the mud is waterlogged and has very little air.",
      example: { q: "Explain why the breathing roots of the Api-api tree grow upwards out of the mud.", marks: 2, think: ["What is special about mangrove mud? It is waterlogged, so there is very little air in it.", "The roots need oxygen to stay alive.", "Growing upwards out of the mud lets the roots reach the air.", "So they can take in oxygen from the air."], answer: "The mud is waterlogged and has very little air. The breathing roots grow out of the mud so they can take in oxygen from the air." },
      tip: "Breathing roots = oxygen; prop roots = support. Do not mix them up.",
      try: { q: "A layer of oil covers the surface of a pond. Which organism will be affected the MOST quickly?", o: ["Guppies, which take in dissolved oxygen through their gills", "Hydrilla plants, which grow rooted at the bottom of the pond", "Snails, which crawl slowly and feed on the pond floor", "Mosquito larvae, which breathe air through tubes at the surface"], a: 3, why: "Mosquito larvae must take in air at the surface through their breathing tubes; the oil blocks this at once. The other organisms take in oxygen dissolved in the water." }
    },
    {
      h: "Explaining changes in a population graph",
      concept: "First DESCRIBE the trend with numbers (increased from … to …, then decreased). Then EXPLAIN with a cause: more or less food, more or fewer predators, or competition. A population rises when births are more than deaths and falls when deaths are more than births.",
      example: { q: "Ladybirds, which eat aphids, were added to a garden at week 4. Describe and explain the change in the aphid population.", fig: { type: "line", title: "Aphid population in a garden", xl: "Week", yl: "Number of aphids", pts: [["0", 50], ["2", 120], ["4", 200], ["6", 110], ["8", 70], ["10", 60]] }, marks: 3, think: ["Describe: from week 0 to 4 the number rose from 50 to 200.", "From week 4 to 10 it fell from 200 to 60.", "Link the change to the event at week 4: ladybirds were added.", "Ladybirds eat aphids, so more aphids died than were born."], answer: "The aphid population increased from 50 to 200 from week 0 to week 4. After week 4 it decreased to 60 because the ladybirds ate the aphids, so more aphids died than were born." },
      tip: "Quote the numbers from the graph, and always link the change to a cause given in the question.",
      try: { q: "Using the same graph, what would most likely happen to the ladybird population after week 10 if the aphids kept decreasing?", fig: { type: "line", title: "Aphid population in a garden", xl: "Week", yl: "Number of aphids", pts: [["0", 50], ["2", 120], ["4", 200], ["6", 110], ["8", 70], ["10", 60]] }, o: ["It would decrease because there would be less food for the ladybirds.", "It would increase because there would be fewer aphids to compete with.", "It would stay the same because ladybirds do not depend on aphids.", "It would increase because ladybirds would have more space."], a: 0, why: "The aphids are the ladybirds’ food. Fewer aphids means less food, so fewer ladybirds survive and reproduce." }
    },
    {
      h: "Physical factors and where organisms are found",
      concept: "Match the organism to the conditions it needs. Read the data, find the spot with the most (or least) of the factor, and explain using the need: plants need light to make food; woodlice need damp, dark places to lose less water and hide from predators. When planning a fair test, change only ONE factor.",
      example: { q: "The graph shows the light intensity at four spots in a school garden. The soil at all four spots is equally moist. Ferns grow best in shady places. At which spot are ferns most likely to be found, and why are sunflowers most likely to grow best at spot P?", fig: { type: "bar", title: "Light intensity at four spots", xl: "Spot", yl: "Light intensity (units)", bars: [["P", 90], ["Q", 40], ["R", 60], ["S", 10]] }, marks: 2, think: ["Ferns need shade: look for the lowest bar, S (10 units).", "Sunflowers need a lot of light: the highest bar is P (90 units).", "Link light to the plant’s need: more light, more photosynthesis.", "So the sunflowers can make more food and grow well."], answer: "Ferns: spot S, because it has the least light. Sunflowers grow best at P because P receives the most light, so they can make more food by photosynthesis." },
      tip: "Say ‘receives the most light, so it can make more food’: the link to making food earns the second mark.",
      try: { q: "Pupils wanted to find out whether woodlice prefer dark places. Which two set-ups should they compare?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "box", lines: ["Dark", "Damp soil"] }, { label: "B", icon: "box", lines: ["Bright", "Damp soil"] }, { label: "C", icon: "box", lines: ["Dark", "Dry soil"] }, { label: "D", icon: "box", lines: ["Bright", "Moist sand"] }] }, o: ["A and C", "B and D", "C and D", "A and B"], a: 3, why: "A and B differ only in the amount of light; both have damp soil. A and C differ in moisture, and C and D differ in more than one factor." }
    }
  ],
  mcq: [
    { q: "Which of the following best describes a habitat?", o: ["The place where an organism lives", "A group of organisms of the same kind", "All the living things in one place", "The food that an organism eats"], a: 0, lvl: 1, why: "A habitat is the place where an organism lives. A group of the same kind is a population, and all the living things together form a community." },
    { q: "Which of the following is a population?", o: ["All the plants and animals in a pond", "All the mudskippers in a mangrove swamp at Sungei Buloh", "A mudskipper and a crab living in the same mud", "All the different kinds of fish in an aquarium"], a: 1, lvl: 1, why: "A population is a group of organisms of the SAME kind living in the same place. The other options contain more than one kind of organism." },
    { q: "Which of the following is a behavioural adaptation?", o: ["A polar bear has a thick layer of fat.", "A cactus has spines.", "Emperor penguins huddle together in the cold.", "A duck has webbed feet."], a: 2, lvl: 1, why: "Huddling is something the penguins DO, so it is behavioural. Fat, spines and webbed feet are body features, so they are structural." },
    { q: "Which of the following is NOT a physical factor of the environment?", o: ["Amount of light", "Temperature", "Amount of mineral salts in the soil", "Number of predators"], a: 3, lvl: 1, why: "Predators are living things. Light, temperature, water, air and mineral salts are the physical (non-living) factors." },
    { q: "The Api-api tree in the mangrove swamp has breathing roots that stick out of the mud. What is the main function of these roots?", o: ["To make food for the tree", "To take in oxygen from the air", "To absorb salt from the sea water", "To attract crabs to the tree"], a: 1, lvl: 1, why: "The mud is waterlogged and has very little air, so the breathing roots grow out of the mud to take in oxygen from the air." },
    { q: "Study the Venn diagram. Which animal lives in water but does NOT breathe with gills?", fig: { type: "venn", a: "Lives in water", b: "Breathes with gills", onlyA: ["dolphin"], both: ["guppy", "tadpole"], onlyB: [], neither: ["cat", "pigeon"] }, o: ["Guppy", "Tadpole", "Dolphin", "Cat"], a: 2, lvl: 1, why: "The dolphin is only in the ‘Lives in water’ circle: it lives in water but breathes with lungs. The guppy and tadpole are in both circles." },
    { q: "Some animals in cold countries go into a deep sleep through the winter, when food is scarce. What is this behaviour called?", o: ["Hibernation", "Migration", "Camouflage", "Mimicry"], a: 0, lvl: 1, why: "Hibernation is a deep sleep in which the body’s activities slow down to save energy. Migration is moving to another place." },
    { q: "A stick insect looks like a twig. How does this help it survive?", o: ["It can make its own food.", "It can move faster than its predators.", "It can take in more oxygen.", "Its predators find it hard to see it."], a: 3, lvl: 1, why: "This is camouflage: the stick insect blends in with the twigs around it, so predators find it hard to see it." },

    { q: "Study the classification key. Which letter could represent a dolphin?", fig: { type: "flow", root: "Animals", node: { q: "Breathes with gills?", yes: { q: "Has scales?", yes: "A", no: "B" }, no: { q: "Lives in water?", yes: "C", no: "D" } } }, o: ["A", "B", "C", "D"], a: 2, lvl: 2, why: "A dolphin breathes with lungs (not gills) and lives in water, so it is C. A would be a fish such as a guppy." },
    { q: "The food web shows ALL the organisms living in a pond. Each letter stands for a different kind of organism, and there are many of each kind. How many populations and how many communities are there in the pond?", fig: { type: "web", links: [["P", "Q"], ["P", "R"], ["Q", "S"], ["R", "S"], ["S", "T"]] }, o: ["5 populations and 1 community", "1 population and 5 communities", "5 populations and 5 communities", "4 populations and 1 community"], a: 0, lvl: 2, why: "There are 5 kinds of organisms (P, Q, R, S and T), so there are 5 populations. Together they form one community in the pond." },
    { q: "The graph shows the light intensity at four spots in a park. The soil at all four spots is equally damp. Moss grows best in damp, shady places. At which spot is moss most likely to be found?", fig: { type: "bar", title: "Light intensity at four spots", xl: "Spot", yl: "Light intensity (units)", bars: [["P", 80], ["Q", 30], ["R", 55], ["S", 5]] }, o: ["P", "Q", "R", "S"], a: 3, lvl: 2, why: "S receives the least light, so it is the shadiest. As all spots are equally damp, light is the deciding factor." },
    { q: "Mosquito larvae are often seen hanging just below the surface of the water. Why do they do this?", o: ["To catch food that falls onto the water", "To take in air through their breathing tubes", "To keep warm in the sunlight", "To hide from fish"], a: 1, lvl: 2, why: "Mosquito larvae breathe through breathing tubes that must reach the water surface to take in air. They do not have gills." },
    { q: "The leaves of a cactus are spines. How does this help the cactus survive in the desert?", o: ["It helps the cactus trap more light.", "It helps the cactus absorb water from the air.", "It reduces water loss from the cactus.", "It attracts animals to disperse its seeds."], a: 2, lvl: 2, why: "Spines have a much smaller surface area than broad leaves, so less water is lost from the cactus in the hot, dry desert." },
    { q: "Study the Venn diagram. Which of the following could be Q?", fig: { type: "venn", a: "Helps escape predators", b: "Behavioural adaptation", onlyA: ["P"], both: ["Q"], onlyB: ["R"], neither: ["S"] }, o: ["A porcupine has sharp quills.", "An opossum plays dead when attacked.", "Birds migrate to warmer places before winter.", "A camel’s hump stores fat."], a: 1, lvl: 2, why: "Q must be a behaviour AND help escape predators: playing dead. Quills are structural (P), migration is a behaviour for surviving winter (R), and the hump is neither (S)." },
    { q: "The harmless hoverfly has yellow and black stripes like a wasp, which can sting. What is this adaptation called and how does it help the hoverfly?", o: ["Camouflage; it blends in with the flowers it feeds on.", "Hibernation; it saves energy when food is scarce.", "Mimicry; it can sting predators like a wasp.", "Mimicry; predators mistake it for a wasp and avoid it."], a: 3, lvl: 2, why: "A harmless organism that looks like a harmful one shows mimicry. The hoverfly cannot sting; predators simply avoid it because they mistake it for a wasp." },
    { q: "The graph shows the number of migratory shorebirds counted at Sungei Buloh Wetland Reserve in different months. Which statement best explains the pattern?", fig: { type: "bar", title: "Migratory shorebirds at Sungei Buloh", xl: "Month", yl: "Number of birds", bars: [["Jul", 20], ["Sep", 300], ["Nov", 900], ["Jan", 850], ["Mar", 400], ["May", 30]] }, o: ["The birds hibernate in Singapore from July to May and wake up only in June.", "The birds hatch in Singapore in November, grow up here and leave in May.", "The birds escape the cold northern winter, when food is scarce there, and leave around March to May.", "The birds come to Singapore in July because it is cooler here then than in the north."], a: 2, lvl: 2, why: "The numbers are high from about September to March, the winter months in the north. The birds migrate to warmer places where food is available, and return north to breed." },
    { q: "The graph shows the aphid population in a garden. Between which weeks did the aphid population decrease the fastest?", fig: { type: "line", title: "Aphid population in a garden", xl: "Week", yl: "Number of aphids", pts: [["0", 50], ["2", 120], ["4", 200], ["6", 110], ["8", 70], ["10", 60]] }, o: ["Week 0 to week 2", "Week 2 to week 4", "Week 8 to week 10", "Week 4 to week 6"], a: 3, lvl: 2, why: "From week 4 to 6 the population fell by 90 (200 to 110), the biggest drop in any 2 weeks. Weeks 0 to 4 show an increase." },
    { q: "The graph shows the temperature of the sand in a desert over one day. The jerboa is a small desert animal that stays in its burrow when the sand is hot. At which time is it most likely to come out to look for food?", fig: { type: "line", title: "Temperature of desert sand", xl: "Time", yl: "Temperature (°C)", pts: [["6am", 15], ["9am", 30], ["12pm", 50], ["3pm", 55], ["6pm", 35], ["9pm", 20]] }, o: ["9 pm", "9 am", "12 pm", "3 pm"], a: 0, lvl: 2, why: "Of the times given, the sand is coolest at 9 pm (20 °C). Being nocturnal helps the jerboa avoid the heat and lose less water." },
    { q: "A water lily has large, flat leaves that float on the surface of the pond. How does this help the water lily?", o: ["It helps the plant absorb more mineral salts.", "It helps the leaves trap more light to make food.", "It protects the plant from fish.", "It helps the plant store water."], a: 1, lvl: 2, why: "Floating leaves are at the surface where the light is, and their large, flat shape lets them trap more light for photosynthesis." },
    { q: "The polar bear has white fur. How does the colour of its fur help it to obtain food?", o: ["It blends in with the snow, so its prey cannot easily see it coming.", "It keeps the polar bear warm while it waits quietly for its prey.", "It makes the polar bear look like a harmful animal to other animals.", "It reflects light so the polar bear can see its prey more clearly."], a: 0, lvl: 2, why: "The white fur is camouflage in the snow, so the polar bear can get close to its prey without being seen. Keeping warm is the job of the thick fur and fat, not its colour." },
    { q: "A dolphin can stay underwater for a long time, but it must swim to the surface regularly. Why?", o: ["To catch fish near the surface", "To warm itself in the sunlight", "To take in oxygen dissolved in the water with its gills", "To take in air through its blowhole into its lungs"], a: 3, lvl: 2, why: "Dolphins have lungs, not gills, so they cannot take in oxygen dissolved in water. They breathe air through the blowhole on top of the head." },
    { q: "Study the food web. Which two organisms compete with each other for food?", fig: { type: "web", links: [["grass", "grasshopper"], ["grass", "rabbit"], ["grasshopper", "frog"], ["frog", "snake"], ["rabbit", "snake"]] }, o: ["Frog and rabbit", "Grasshopper and rabbit", "Snake and grasshopper", "Grass and frog"], a: 1, lvl: 2, why: "Both the grasshopper and the rabbit feed on the grass, so they compete for it. The frog and rabbit eat different things." },

    { q: "Study the set-ups. Twenty woodlice were placed in a container divided into four sections A to D. Which two sections should be compared to find out whether woodlice prefer damp places?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "box", lines: ["Dark", "Damp soil"] }, { label: "B", icon: "box", lines: ["Bright", "Damp soil"] }, { label: "C", icon: "box", lines: ["Dark", "Dry soil"] }, { label: "D", icon: "box", lines: ["Bright", "Moist sand"] }] }, o: ["A and B", "B and D", "A and C", "C and D"], a: 2, lvl: 3, why: "Only the moisture must differ. A and C are both dark with soil; only damp vs dry changes. B and D differ in both moisture and the type of soil." },
    { q: "Study the set-ups. The same kind of seeds were sown in two identical pots of soil and given the same amount of water and light. After three weeks, the seedlings in pot B were shorter with smaller, yellowish leaves. Which is the best explanation?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "pot", lines: ["5 seeds", "Pot 20 cm wide"] }, { label: "B", icon: "pot", lines: ["30 seeds", "Pot 20 cm wide"] }] }, o: ["The seedlings in B competed with one another for water, mineral salts and space.", "The seedlings in B received too much light, which burnt their young leaves.", "The seedlings in B had fewer predators, so they did not need to grow tall.", "The seedlings in B each had more mineral salts, which turned the leaves yellow."], a: 0, lvl: 3, why: "In B, 30 seedlings share the same amount of soil, water and space as 5 seedlings in A, so each gets less. This competition makes them grow poorly." },
    { q: "Ali saw these organisms at a pond in Sungei Buloh: 3 kingfishers, 20 mudskippers, 15 fiddler crabs, many Api-api trees and many Bakau trees. How many populations did he see?", o: ["2", "4", "5", "38"], a: 2, lvl: 3, why: "Count the KINDS: kingfisher, mudskipper, fiddler crab, Api-api and Bakau = 5 populations. The two kinds of trees are separate populations." },
    { q: "Which of the following statements about a community are correct?\nA: A community is made up of different populations.\nB: All the organisms in a community are of the same kind.\nC: The organisms in a community interact with one another.\nD: A community can include plants, animals, fungi and bacteria.", o: ["A and C only", "A, C and D only", "B and D only", "B, C and D only"], a: 1, lvl: 3, why: "B describes a population, not a community. A community is all the different populations (plants, animals, fungi, bacteria) living and interacting in one place." },
    { q: "Which of the following help mangrove trees survive in soft, waterlogged mud?\nA: Prop roots that support the tree\nB: Breathing roots that take in oxygen from the air\nC: Seeds that germinate while still on the parent tree\nD: Roots that absorb oxygen from the mud", o: ["A and B only", "B and D only", "A, B, C and D", "A, B and C only"], a: 3, lvl: 3, why: "Waterlogged mud has very little air, which is why breathing roots grow out into the air, so D is wrong. Prop roots give support, and the Bakau seedling can take root quickly when it drops into the mud." },
    { q: "A pond has snails that feed on water plants. The graph shows the snail population after fish that eat snails were added to the pond at month 3. What most likely happened to the population of water plants from month 3 to month 6?", fig: { type: "line", title: "Snail population in a pond", xl: "Month", yl: "Number of snails", pts: [["0", 40], ["1", 60], ["2", 80], ["3", 90], ["4", 60], ["5", 35], ["6", 30]] }, o: ["It increased, because fewer snails were feeding on the water plants.", "It decreased, because the fish ate the water plants.", "It stayed the same, because the fish do not eat water plants.", "It decreased, because there were fewer snails."], a: 0, lvl: 3, why: "After month 3 the snails decreased as the fish ate them. With fewer snails eating them, more water plants survive, so their population increases. This shows interdependence." },
    { q: "Which of the following are behavioural adaptations?\nA: A turtle pulls its head into its shell when it senses danger.\nB: A porcupine has sharp quills.\nC: An opossum plays dead when attacked.\nD: Some birds fly to warmer countries before winter.", o: ["A and C only", "A, C and D only", "B and D only", "C only"], a: 1, lvl: 3, why: "Pulling in the head, playing dead and migrating are things the animals DO. The quills are a body feature, so B is structural. (The turtle’s shell is structural, but pulling the head in is behavioural.)" },
    { q: "Pupils counted woodlice at four spots. Spot A: sunny and dry. Spot B: shady and dry. Spot C: sunny and damp. Spot D: shady and damp. Study the graph. Which conclusion is best supported?", fig: { type: "bar", title: "Woodlice found at four spots", xl: "Spot", yl: "Number of woodlice", bars: [["A", 2], ["B", 9], ["C", 6], ["D", 25]] }, o: ["Woodlice prefer bright, dry places.", "Only moisture affects where woodlice are found.", "Woodlice prefer shady, damp places.", "Only light affects where woodlice are found."], a: 2, lvl: 3, why: "B (shady) has more than A (sunny), so light matters; C (damp) has more than A (dry), so moisture matters. Most are at D, which is both shady and damp." },
    { q: "The Arctic fox, which lives in the cold Arctic, has small ears. The fennec fox, which lives in the hot desert, has large ears. Which is the best explanation?", o: ["Small ears lose less heat, keeping the Arctic fox warm; large ears lose more heat, keeping the fennec fox cool.", "Small ears help the Arctic fox hear prey under the snow; large ears keep sand out of the fennec fox’s eyes.", "Large ears store water for the fennec fox in the desert; small ears store fat for the Arctic fox.", "Small ears gain more heat from the Sun for the Arctic fox; large ears block sunlight from the fennec fox."], a: 0, lvl: 3, why: "Heat is lost from the surface of the ears. Small ears have less surface to lose heat (useful in the cold), while large ears lose more heat (useful in the heat)." },
    { q: "Which of the following shows a PLANT depending on an ANIMAL?", o: ["Plants give off oxygen that animals take in.", "Caterpillars feed on the leaves of plants.", "Birds build their nests in the branches of trees.", "Bees carry pollen from flower to flower."], a: 3, lvl: 3, why: "The plant depends on the bee to pollinate its flowers. In the other options, the animal depends on the plant for oxygen, food or shelter." },
    { q: "The graph shows the guppy population in a pond. Which statement is correct?", fig: { type: "line", title: "Guppy population in a pond", xl: "Month", yl: "Number of guppies", pts: [["Jan", 40], ["Feb", 55], ["Mar", 70], ["Apr", 65], ["May", 50], ["Jun", 52]] }, o: ["More guppies were born than died from January to March.", "No guppies died from January to March.", "The guppy population was largest in June.", "More guppies were born than died from March to May."], a: 0, lvl: 3, why: "The population increased from January to March, so births were more than deaths. It does not mean no guppies died. From March to May it decreased, so deaths were more than births." },
    { q: "The Angsana fruit has a flat, wing-like part around the seed. How does this help the Angsana to reproduce successfully?", o: ["The fruit is heavy, so it drops right below the parent tree and grows there.", "The fruit floats on water, so it can be carried to other islands by the sea.", "The wing attracts birds, which eat the fruit and drop the seeds elsewhere.", "The wind carries the fruit far from the parent tree, so there is less competition."], a: 3, lvl: 3, why: "The wing lets the fruit be dispersed by wind. Seeds that land away from the parent tree do not have to compete with it for light, water, mineral salts and space." },

    { q: "In a pond, fish X feeds only on snails, and the snails feed on Hydrilla, the only water plant in the pond. Herons eat fish X, which hides among the Hydrilla. All the Hydrilla was removed and the population of fish X decreased. Which statements help to explain this?\nA: Less oxygen was given off into the water.\nB: The snails had less food, so fish X had less food.\nC: Fish X had fewer places to hide from herons.\nD: Fish X had to compete with the snails for Hydrilla.", o: ["A and B only", "A, B and C only", "B and D only", "A, B, C and D"], a: 1, lvl: 4, why: "Hydrilla gives off oxygen, is food for the snails (fish X’s food), and gives fish X shelter, so A, B and C all apply. D is wrong: fish X does not eat Hydrilla, so it does not compete with snails for it." },
    { q: "After an oil spill in a mangrove swamp, the breathing roots of many Api-api trees were coated with a thick layer of oil. Many of these trees later died. Which is the best explanation?", o: ["The roots could no longer absorb mineral salts from the mud.", "The leaves could no longer trap enough light to make food for the tree.", "The breathing roots could no longer take in oxygen from the air.", "The trees lost their support and fell over in the soft mud."], a: 2, lvl: 4, why: "The oil covered the breathing roots. Since the waterlogged mud has very little air, the roots depend on the breathing roots for oxygen. Support is the job of prop roots, and the leaves were not coated." },
    { q: "Species Y is a small bird that lives on an island. In 2021, a new bird, species X, which eats the same insects as species Y, was brought to the island. Study the graph of the population of species Y. Which statement is best supported?", fig: { type: "line", title: "Population of species Y", xl: "Year", yl: "Number of birds", pts: [["2019", 500], ["2020", 520], ["2021", 510], ["2022", 380], ["2023", 260], ["2024", 250]] }, o: ["Species X eats species Y, so species Y decreased steadily after 2021.", "Species X competed with Y for the same insects, so Y decreased, then levelled off.", "Species Y decreased because species X brought more insects to the island.", "Species Y increased after species X arrived, as X helped it find insects."], a: 1, lvl: 4, why: "Both birds eat the same insects, so they compete for food. Species Y fell from 510 to 250 after 2021 and then levelled off. Nothing says X eats Y." },
    { q: "Study the classification key. Which statement MUST be true?", fig: { type: "flow", root: "Animals", node: { q: "Has lungs?", yes: { q: "Lives mainly in water?", yes: "P", no: "Q" }, no: { q: "Has gills?", yes: "R", no: "S" } } }, o: ["P has both gills and lungs to breathe.", "Q cannot live in water at all.", "S must live on land, not in water.", "R can take in oxygen dissolved in water."], a: 3, lvl: 4, why: "R has gills, and gills take in oxygen dissolved in water. P need not have gills (e.g. a dolphin), Q may still swim, and S could be a water animal such as a mosquito larva that breathes through a breathing tube." },
    { q: "Which features of a cactus help it REDUCE WATER LOSS?\nA: Spines instead of broad leaves\nB: A thick, waxy layer on the stem\nC: Long roots that spread widely near the surface\nD: A thick, fleshy stem that stores water", o: ["A and B only", "A, B and D only", "C and D only", "A, B, C and D"], a: 0, lvl: 4, why: "Spines and a waxy layer reduce the water lost. Widespread roots help ABSORB more water, and the fleshy stem STORES water; both help survival but do not reduce water loss." },
    { q: "Pupils counted plant Z in five areas that had different light intensities. All other conditions were the same. Study the graph. Which conclusion is best supported?", fig: { type: "bar", title: "Number of plant Z found", xl: "Light intensity (units)", yl: "Number of plant Z", bars: [["10", 2], ["30", 8], ["50", 20], ["70", 18], ["90", 6]] }, o: ["Plant Z grows best where there is the most light, so it needs full sunlight.", "Light intensity has no effect on plant Z, as it was found in all five areas.", "Plant Z grows best at medium to high light, with fewer at both the lowest and highest levels.", "Plant Z cannot grow at a light intensity of 10 units because it is too dark."], a: 2, lvl: 4, why: "Most plants were found at 50 to 70 units, with fewer at 10 and at 90. So ‘more light is always better’ is wrong, and 2 plants were found at 10 units, so it CAN grow there." }
  ],
  tf: [
    { s: "All the fish in a pond always form one population.", a: false, why: "Only fish of the same kind form a population; a pond with guppies and goldfish has two fish populations." },
    { s: "A community is made up of different populations living together in the same place.", a: true, why: "This is the definition of a community." },
    { s: "The plants in a pond are part of the pond community.", a: true, why: "A community includes all the populations, including plants, fungi and bacteria." },
    { s: "A camel’s hump stores water for long journeys.", a: false, why: "The hump stores fat, which can be used for energy when food is scarce." },
    { s: "Light, temperature, water, air and mineral salts are physical factors of the environment.", a: true, why: "These are the non-living factors that affect where organisms live." },
    { s: "The breathing roots of mangrove trees take in water from the mud.", a: false, why: "They grow out of the waterlogged mud to take in oxygen from the air." },
    { s: "Prop roots help to support mangrove trees in the soft mud.", a: true, why: "Prop roots grow from the trunk down into the mud, supporting the tree." },
    { s: "Whales and dolphins breathe with gills.", a: false, why: "They breathe with lungs and take in air through their blowholes at the surface." },
    { s: "Playing dead is a structural adaptation.", a: false, why: "It is something the animal does, so it is a behavioural adaptation." },
    { s: "Camouflage helps an animal to blend in with its surroundings.", a: true, why: "Blending in makes it hard for predators (or prey) to see the animal." },
    { s: "Mimicry is when an animal changes colour to match its surroundings.", a: false, why: "That is camouflage. Mimicry is a harmless organism looking like a harmful one." },
    { s: "Animals that are nocturnal are active at night.", a: true, why: "Many desert animals are nocturnal to avoid the heat of the day." },
    { s: "Migrating birds come to Singapore mainly to hibernate.", a: false, why: "They migrate to escape the cold northern winter and find food; they stay active here." },
    { s: "Plants growing close together compete for light, water, mineral salts and space.", a: true, why: "These resources are limited, so crowded plants each get less." },
    { s: "Seeds are dispersed so that they will grow right next to the parent plant.", a: false, why: "Dispersal carries seeds AWAY from the parent plant, so they compete less with it." },
    { s: "A population always increases when there is more food.", a: false, why: "Not always: more predators, disease or less space could still make it decrease. More food only tends to increase it." },
    { s: "Plants and animals in a community depend on one another.", a: true, why: "Plants give food, oxygen and shelter; animals pollinate flowers, disperse seeds and give off carbon dioxide." },
    { s: "The spines of a cactus help it to reduce water loss.", a: true, why: "Spines have a much smaller surface area than broad leaves, so less water is lost." },
    { s: "A habitat and a community mean the same thing.", a: false, why: "A habitat is the place; a community is the group of different populations living in that place." },
    { s: "Mosquito larvae breathe with gills.", a: false, why: "They take in air at the water surface through breathing tubes." }
  ],
  sort: [
    { title: "Sort these adaptations into structural and behavioural", groups: ["Structural", "Behavioural"], items: [["polar bear’s thick fur", 0], ["cactus spines", 0], ["breathing roots of Api-api", 0], ["hoverfly’s wasp-like stripes", 0], ["duck’s webbed feet", 0], ["penguins huddling together", 1], ["birds migrating before winter", 1], ["opossum playing dead", 1], ["jerboa staying in its burrow by day", 1], ["bear hibernating in winter", 1]] },
    { title: "What does each adaptation mainly help the organism to do?", groups: ["Obtain food", "Escape predators", "Survive extreme conditions", "Breathe in water"], items: [["eagle’s hooked beak", 0], ["frog’s long sticky tongue", 0], ["pitcher plant trapping insects", 0], ["stick insect looking like a twig", 1], ["squid squirting ink", 1], ["tortoise’s hard shell", 1], ["camel’s long eyelashes", 2], ["polar bear’s thick layer of fat", 2], ["fish’s gills", 3], ["mosquito larva’s breathing tube", 3]] },
    { title: "At a pond in Sungei Buloh: organism, population or community?", groups: ["Organism", "Population", "Community"], items: [["one kingfisher", 0], ["a single Bakau tree", 0], ["one fiddler crab", 0], ["all the mudskippers in the pond", 1], ["all the Api-api trees in the pond", 1], ["all the fiddler crabs in the pond", 1], ["all the plants, animals and microorganisms in the pond", 2], ["all the different populations in the pond", 2]] }
  ],
  oe: [
    { q: "A pond in the mangrove swamp at Sungei Buloh has mudskippers, fiddler crabs, kingfishers and mangrove trees.\n(a) Explain why all the mudskippers in the pond form a population. [1]\n(b) Explain why all the organisms in the pond form a community. [1]\n(c) What is the habitat of the mudskippers? [1]", marks: 3, kw: [["same kind"], ["different populations", "different kinds", "populations of"], ["pond", "mangrove"]], model: "(a) They are a group of organisms of the same kind living together in the same place at the same time. (b) They are different populations of organisms living together and interacting with one another in the same place. (c) The pond in the mangrove swamp." },
    { q: "Mangrove swamps have soft, waterlogged mud.\n(a) The Api-api tree has breathing roots that grow upwards out of the mud. Explain how these roots help the tree survive. [2]\n(b) The Bakau tree has prop roots. State how prop roots help the tree survive. [1]", marks: 3, kw: [["take in oxygen", "take in air", "obtain oxygen", "get oxygen", "absorb oxygen"], ["waterlogged", "little air", "lack of air", "little oxygen"], ["support", "anchor", "hold the tree"]], model: "(a) The mud is waterlogged and has very little air. The breathing roots grow out of the mud so that they can take in oxygen from the air. (b) The prop roots support the tree in the soft mud." },
    { q: "Twenty woodlice were placed in the centre of a container divided into four sections as shown. After 30 minutes, most of the woodlice were in section A.\n(a) Based on the result, state the two conditions woodlice prefer. [2]\n(b) Explain how living in such conditions helps woodlice survive. [2]", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "box", lines: ["Dark", "Damp soil"] }, { label: "B", icon: "box", lines: ["Bright", "Damp soil"] }, { label: "C", icon: "box", lines: ["Dark", "Dry soil"] }, { label: "D", icon: "box", lines: ["Bright", "Dry soil"] }] }, marks: 4, kw: [["dark", "shady", "dim"], ["damp", "moist", "wet"], ["lose less water", "less water loss", "reduce water loss", "lose water less"], ["predator", "hide", "hidden", "seen"]], model: "(a) Woodlice prefer dark and damp places. (b) In damp places they lose less water from their bodies, and in dark places they can hide from predators more easily." },
    { q: "Ladybirds, which eat aphids, were added to a garden at week 4. The graph shows the aphid population.\n(a) Describe how the aphid population changed from week 0 to week 4. [1]\n(b) Describe how it changed from week 4 to week 10. [1]\n(c) Explain the change after week 4. [1]\n(d) Predict what would happen to the ladybird population if the aphids continued to decrease. Explain. [1]", fig: { type: "line", title: "Aphid population in a garden", xl: "Week", yl: "Number of aphids", pts: [["0", 50], ["2", 120], ["4", 200], ["6", 110], ["8", 70], ["10", 60]] }, marks: 4, kw: [["increase", "rose", "went up"], ["decrease", "fell", "dropped", "went down"], ["ladybirds ate", "eat the aphids", "ate the aphids", "eaten", "eats the aphids", "feed on"], ["less food", "fewer aphids", "food is scarce", "less aphids"]], model: "(a) It increased from 50 to 200. (b) It decreased from 200 to 60. (c) The ladybirds ate the aphids, so more aphids died than were born. (d) The ladybird population would decrease because there would be less food for the ladybirds.", x: 1 },
    { q: "The graph shows the light intensity at four spots in a school garden: an open field, beside a wall, under a bush and under a big rain tree. The soil at all four spots is the same.\n(a) At which spot is moss, which grows best in shady places, most likely to be found? [1]\n(b) Sunflowers grow best in the open field. Explain why. [2]", fig: { type: "bar", title: "Light intensity at four spots", xl: "Spot", yl: "Light intensity (units)", bars: [["Field", 90], ["Wall", 60], ["Bush", 35], ["Tree", 10]] }, marks: 3, kw: [["tree"], ["most light", "more light", "greatest amount of light", "highest light", "brightest"], ["make more food", "photosynth", "make food"]], model: "(a) Under the big rain tree. (b) The open field receives the most light, so the sunflowers can make more food by photosynthesis and grow well." },
    { q: "Camels live in hot, sandy deserts.\n(a) A camel has long eyelashes. Explain how this helps it during a sandstorm. [1]\n(b) A camel’s hump stores fat. Explain how this helps the camel survive a long journey across the desert. [2]", marks: 3, kw: [["keep sand out", "keeps sand out", "prevent sand", "protect its eyes", "protects its eyes", "sand from entering"], ["energy"], ["food is scarce", "little food", "scarce", "lack of food", "without food"]], model: "(a) The long eyelashes keep sand out of its eyes. (b) The fat can be broken down to give the camel energy when food is scarce during the long journey." },
    { q: "A cactus has spines instead of broad leaves, a thick fleshy stem and long roots that spread widely just below the surface of the sand. Explain how EACH of these features helps the cactus survive in the desert. [3]", marks: 3, kw: [["reduce water loss", "less water loss", "lose less water", "reduces the loss of water", "reducing water loss"], ["store water", "stores water", "storing water"], ["absorb", "take in water", "takes in water"]], model: "The spines reduce water loss from the cactus. The thick, fleshy stem stores water for the dry periods. The widespread roots can absorb water quickly over a large area when it rains." },
    { q: "Mosquito larvae are often seen hanging just below the surface of the water in a pond.\n(a) Explain why they stay at the water surface. [2]\n(b) Spreading a thin layer of oil on the water surface kills the larvae. Explain why. [1]", marks: 3, kw: [["breathing tube"], ["air", "oxygen"], ["block", "cover", "prevent", "cannot take in", "unable to take in", "stop"]], model: "(a) They have breathing tubes which reach the surface to take in air. (b) The oil forms a layer that blocks the breathing tubes, so the larvae cannot take in air and they die." },
    { q: "Winters in some northern countries are very cold and food is hard to find.\n(a) Some birds fly to places like Singapore before winter. State two reasons why. [2]\n(b) Some other animals hibernate through the winter. Explain how this helps them survive. [1]", marks: 3, kw: [["warm"], ["food"], ["save energy", "conserve energy", "use less energy", "less energy"]], model: "(a) They migrate to escape the cold winter to warmer places, where there is more food. (b) During hibernation their body activities slow down so they use less energy and can survive until food is available again." },
    { q: "The same kind of seeds were sown in two identical pots of soil as shown and given the same amount of light and water. After 3 weeks, the seedlings in pot B were shorter and had smaller, yellowish leaves.\n(a) Explain why the seedlings in pot B grew poorly. [1]\n(b) Name one resource, other than space, that the seedlings in pot B competed for. [1]\n(c) Suggest one change to pot B so that its seedlings would grow as well as those in pot A. [1]", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "pot", lines: ["5 seeds", "Pot 20 cm wide"] }, { label: "B", icon: "pot", lines: ["30 seeds", "Pot 20 cm wide"] }] }, marks: 3, kw: [["compete", "competition"], ["water", "light", "mineral salts", "nutrients"], ["fewer seeds", "sow fewer", "plant fewer", "remove", "thin", "larger pot", "bigger pot", "wider pot", "5 seeds", "five seeds"]], model: "(a) The seedlings in pot B were crowded together, so they had to compete with one another for resources. (b) Water (each seedling also got less light and mineral salts). (c) Sow fewer seeds in pot B, such as 5 seeds, or use a larger pot." },
    { q: "The Angsana tree produces fruits with a flat, wing-like part around the seed.\n(a) State how the fruit is dispersed. [1]\n(b) Explain why it is important for the seeds to be dispersed away from the parent tree. [2]", marks: 3, kw: [["wind"], ["compete", "competition"], ["light", "water", "mineral salts", "space"]], model: "(a) By wind. (b) If the seeds grow away from the parent tree, the seedlings will compete less with the parent tree and with one another for light, water, mineral salts and space, so they are more likely to survive." },
    { q: "Study the classification key. A dolphin is placed at C.\n(a) What does its position in the key tell you about how a dolphin breathes? [1]\n(b) Explain why a dolphin must swim to the water surface regularly. [1]", fig: { type: "flow", root: "Animals", node: { q: "Breathes with gills?", yes: { q: "Has scales?", yes: "A", no: "B" }, no: { q: "Lives in water?", yes: "C", no: "D" } } }, marks: 2, kw: [["lung"], ["air", "oxygen from the air"]], model: "(a) The dolphin breathes with lungs, as it does not breathe with gills. (b) It must take in air through its blowhole at the surface, because it has no gills to take in oxygen dissolved in the water." },
    { q: "The food web shows some organisms in a rice field. The farmers killed all the frogs.\n(a) State what would happen to the grasshopper population at first. Explain. [2]\n(b) Suggest why the rat population might later decrease. [1]", fig: { type: "web", links: [["rice plant", "grasshopper"], ["rice plant", "rat"], ["grasshopper", "frog"], ["frog", "snake"], ["rat", "snake"]] }, marks: 3, kw: [["increase"], ["no frogs", "fewer predators", "no predator", "no longer eaten", "frogs eat", "frogs to eat", "predator"], ["less food", "fewer rice plants", "compete", "eat more rats", "more rats are eaten"]], model: "(a) The grasshopper population would increase at first because there are no frogs to eat them. (b) With more grasshoppers eating the rice plants, the rats would have less food as they compete with the grasshoppers for rice plants. Also, the snakes would eat more rats since there are no frogs for them to eat.", x: 1 },
    { q: "Pupils measured the light intensity and the average leaf size of plants of the same kind growing at three spots.\n(a) Describe the relationship between light intensity and leaf size. [1]\n(b) Explain how this helps the plants at the shadiest spot survive. [2]", tbl: [["Spot", "Light intensity (units)", "Average leaf area (cm²)"], ["Open lawn", "90", "20"], ["Edge of forest", "50", "35"], ["Forest floor", "15", "60"]], marks: 3, kw: [["lower the light", "less light", "lower light", "dimmer", "shadier", "decreases"], ["trap more light", "absorb more light", "capture more light", "trap as much light"], ["make enough food", "photosynth", "make more food", "make food"]], model: "(a) The lower the light intensity, the larger the leaves. (b) Larger leaves have a larger surface area to trap more light, so the plants on the dim forest floor can still make enough food by photosynthesis.", x: 1 }
  ],
  doc: [
    { q: "At a pond, there are 20 guppies, 10 goldfish and some water lilies. Explain why ‘all the fish in the pond’ is NOT one population.", marks: 1, answers: ["Because there are 30 fish.", "Because the guppies and goldfish are different kinds of fish, and a population is made up of organisms of the same kind.", "Because the fish live with the water lilies."], best: 1, why: "Answer 2 uses the definition: a population is one kind of organism, and there are two kinds of fish. Answer 1 counts individuals, which does not matter. Answer 3 describes the community, not why the fish are not one population." },
    { q: "Explain how breathing roots help the Api-api tree survive in the mangrove swamp.", marks: 2, answers: ["The mud is waterlogged and has very little air, so the breathing roots grow out of the mud to take in oxygen from the air.", "The breathing roots help the tree breathe.", "The breathing roots hold the tree firmly in the soft mud."], best: 0, why: "Answer 1 gives the problem (waterlogged mud with little air) and the function (take in oxygen from the air). Answer 2 is too vague: it does not say where the oxygen comes from or why. Answer 3 describes prop roots, not breathing roots." },
    { q: "Explain how the white fur of the polar bear helps it catch its prey.", marks: 2, answers: ["The white fur keeps the polar bear warm.", "The polar bear is white so it can survive.", "The white fur helps it blend in with the snow, so its prey cannot easily see it approaching."], best: 2, why: "Answer 3 names the adaptation (blending in, camouflage) and links it to catching prey. Answer 1 gives the job of thick fur, not its white colour. Answer 2 has no reason at all." },
    { q: "Explain why seedlings growing very close together grow poorly.", marks: 2, answers: ["They are too close.", "They compete with one another for water, mineral salts, light and space, so each seedling gets less.", "They do not have enough sunlight."], best: 1, why: "Answer 2 uses the key word ‘compete’ and names the resources. Answer 1 only repeats the question. Answer 3 names one resource but does not explain that crowding causes competition." },
    { q: "A population of rabbits decreased after foxes were brought into the area. Explain why.", marks: 2, answers: ["The foxes did not like the rabbits.", "The rabbits ran away to hibernate.", "The foxes are predators that ate the rabbits, so more rabbits died than were born."], best: 2, why: "Answer 3 names the cause (predation) and links it to deaths being more than births. Answer 1 is not scientific. Answer 2 wrongly uses hibernation, which is sleeping through winter, not running away." }
  ]
};
